# [[ .Name ]] on AWS (ECS Fargate)

Terraform for the Chautari stack of **[[ or .Estate.Org .Estate.Name ]]**,
generated by `chautari generate` from `estate.cdl`. It deploys into a VPC you
already have.

```
  clients ── allowed_cidrs ──▶ ALB :80/:443  (health check /healthz)
                                 │
                                 ▼ :8080 only
  ECS Fargate task  (x desired_count, private subnets)
    render-gateway   init, essential=false: CDL ─▶ /gateway/[[ .GatewayConfigFile ]]
    chautari  :8080  checkpoint, admin API, UI; rewrites the config live
    gateway   :3000 :3100  [[ .Dataplane ]], task-local, holds provider keys
                                 │
                                 ▼ :5432
  RDS PostgreSQL 16  (encrypted, Multi-AZ, deletion protection)
```

## What it creates

* ECS cluster (Container Insights on), Fargate service and task definition with
  three containers sharing an ephemeral volume at `/gateway`:
  `render-gateway` renders the data plane config from the CDL alone;
  `chautari` starts once it succeeds; `gateway` starts once `chautari` is
  healthy (`chautari healthcheck`). Deployments use the circuit breaker with
  rollback.
* Application Load Balancer and target group (`/healthz`). HTTPS on 443 when
  `certificate_arn` is set (80 redirects), plain HTTP on 80 otherwise.
* Security groups: ALB from `allowed_cidrs`; tasks only from the ALB on 8080;
  Postgres only from the tasks. The data plane ports are never opened.
* RDS PostgreSQL 16: encrypted, `rds.force_ssl`, Multi-AZ (`db_multi_az`),
  deletion protection and a final snapshot by default, storage autoscaling.
* Secrets Manager: admin token and gateway key (random), the database URL,
  and one placeholder secret per provider credential
  ([[ if .ProviderEnvs ]][[ range $i, $e := .ProviderEnvs ]][[ if $i ]], [[ end ]]`[[ $e ]]`[[ end ]][[ else ]]none for this estate[[ end ]]).
* SSM parameter (Advanced tier, up to 8 KB) holding `estate.cdl`, injected as
  `CHAUTARI_ESTATE_CDL`.
* IAM: an execution role that can pull images, write logs and read exactly
  those secrets and the parameter; a task role
  [[- if .UsesBedrock ]] allowed to call `bedrock:InvokeModel`,
  `bedrock:InvokeModelWithResponseStream` and `bedrock:ApplyGuardrail`
  (this estate uses Bedrock)[[ else ]] with no permissions (this estate does
  not use AWS Bedrock)[[ end ]].
* A CloudWatch log group, `/ecs/<name>`.

## Prerequisites

* A VPC with at least two private subnets (tasks, RDS) and two subnets for the
  load balancer, in different availability zones.
* Egress from the private subnets to ECR (or wherever the images live), Secrets
  Manager, SSM, CloudWatch Logs and your model providers: a NAT gateway, or VPC
  endpoints plus whatever the providers need.
* Terraform >= 1.6 and credentials for the target account.

## Deploy

```sh
cd terraform/aws
cp terraform.tfvars.example terraform.tfvars   # set VPC, subnets, certificate
terraform init
terraform apply
```
[[- if .ProviderEnvs ]]

Then set the provider credentials (Terraform creates them with the placeholder
`REPLACE_ME` and never overwrites what you put there) and roll the service:

```sh
[[- range .ProviderEnvs ]]
aws secretsmanager put-secret-value \
  --secret-id "$(terraform output -json provider_secret_arns | jq -r '.[[ . ]]')" \
  --secret-string "$[[ . ]]"
[[- end ]]
aws ecs update-service --cluster "$(terraform output -raw ecs_cluster_name)" \
  --service "$(terraform output -raw ecs_service_name)" --force-new-deployment
```
[[- end ]]

## Use it

```sh
URL=$(terraform output -raw admin_url)
ADMIN=$(aws secretsmanager get-secret-value \
  --secret-id "$(terraform output -raw admin_token_secret_arn)" \
  --query SecretString --output text)

# Mint an API key for an app declared in the estate (shown once).
curl -sS -X POST "$URL/api/keys" -H "Authorization: Bearer $ADMIN" \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" -d '{"app": "[[ with .Estate.Apps ]][[ (index . 0).Name ]][[ else ]]my-app[[ end ]]"}'
```

## Changing the estate

Edit `estate.cdl`, run `chautari generate`, review, then `terraform apply`. The
parameter is updated and the task definition carries the estate's SHA-256, so
the service rolls onto the new estate.

## Notes

* State holds the generated passwords and tokens. Use an encrypted remote
  backend (for example S3 with a DynamoDB lock table) before going to production.
* `deletion_protection = true` protects both the database and the load
  balancer; set it to false and apply before `terraform destroy`.
* The database password is generated without special characters because it
  is embedded in the connection URL.
